Before going for the PCB power trace optimization, I annotated and footprinted the schematic with standard footprints after some online research.
I was not entirely sure what footprints to give as I did not have much idea about the availability of components to actually make the PCB so I did my best to choose them after some research.

I also ensured that I added PWR_FLAGs and the +V and -V part of the LM393 comparator after receiving warnings and errors on running the electrical rules checker

PCB trace optimization-

The copper traces carrying signals don’t need any change in thickness as they carry very low current and the default is more than sufficient to handle the current through them.

For the power traces, I measured the current through them via an ammeter in the proteus simulation and obtained a max of approximately 7A in the phases/ overcurrent protection circuit. I obtained a value of approximately 2.2mm width which is what I used.

Using standard 1oz copper, the traces were too thick to route on the PCB. Hence, I made a decision to use 2oz copper instead which significantly brought down the required width of the traces.
To calculate the required width I input the max current, and the thickness (70mum for 2oz and 35mum for 1oz) while retaining the standard delta of 10 degree C change in temperature to obtain the required width from the KiCAD calculator itself.

Then I used 2 layers to make all the connections according to my schematic using an 80mm by 80mm edge cut for the PCB.

I made sure to make all the connections guided by the ratsnest lines provided by the software.
I made sure to leave sufficient space near the MOSFETs and Conn_01x03 for the thick power traces to be comfortably routed which I had not left space for initially and learnt by experience.

After completing the connections, I ran the design rules check to ensure my PCB was fine and checked the 3D view.

Note on current spike-

A very short current spik reaching approximately 8A was observed, too short to capture on an ammeter. I read that copper trace heating generally depends on sustained rather than instantaneous current,and it was impractically thick. Power trace widths are based on the maximum measured sustained current (~7A).
